# ============================================================================
#  HARD EXCLUSIONS — these must NEVER be committed (per project requirements)
# ============================================================================
# The source research report and the private business-strategy reports (confidential, never publish).
#
# By extension, not by name. Those documents live one directory ABOVE this repository and have never
# been inside it, so the five Japanese-named patterns that used to sit here were guarding a path that
# does not exist - while being the only Japanese left in any tracked text file. A bare `*.docx` is
# strictly stronger: it catches every one of them, plus any future report whatever it is called or
# whether it is transliterated. No `.docx` is tracked, so nothing legitimate is shadowed.
#
# Case-folded by hand: gitignore matching is case-SENSITIVE on Linux, so a plain `*.docx` lets
# `Report.DOCX` straight through - verified with `git check-ignore`. Word will happily write either.
*.[dD][oO][cC][xX]
# Copyrighted reference book (do not redistribute)
references/The Business of Quantum Technologies.pdf
# Large third-party research PDFs (Round 16, ~35 MB total) — cited in references/REFERENCES.md, not redistributed
references/OptiQKD_*.pdf
references/AML-QKD_*.pdf
references/Information-Theoretic_Solutions_for_Seedless_QRNG_*.pdf
# Commercial / monetization notes (kept local only — both the EN plan and JA workflow)
MONETIZATION*.md
# The browser verification checklist: still maintained, no longer published (Round 28).
#
# It is the round-by-round verification run sheet, and it accumulated the deploy procedure, the
# internal failure history and the reasoning behind every check. That is the right shape for a working
# document and the wrong shape for a public one, so it stays on disk and out of the repository.
#
# Two honest limits on what this achieves. It was tracked across 69 commits, so the history still
# contains it; removing that would need a rewrite and a force-push, which this project does not do.
# And `report/build_data.py` measures its row count — that now falls back to the committed value in
# `report/generated/macros.tex`, so a fresh clone still builds the report.
docs/browser-verification-checklist.md
# HAZARD, learned the hard way in Round 29: an ignored file is NOT protected from git. Switching to a
# branch or commit where this file was still TRACKED restores the tracked copy over the working one,
# and pulling past the commit that untracked it then deletes the result. That is exactly how a round's
# worth of edits to this file was lost - the commit message described changes that, because the file
# was already untracked, had never been committed. Keep a copy outside the repository
# (~/.local/share/qsh-private/) and re-copy it after any branch switch.

# ============================================================================
#  Python
# ============================================================================
.venv/
venv/
env/.venv/
__pycache__/
*.py[cod]
*$py.class
*.egg-info/
.eggs/
build/
dist/
.pytest_cache/
.mypy_cache/
.ruff_cache/
.ipynb_checkpoints/
*.so

# ============================================================================
#  Data, models, run artifacts (large / regenerable)
# ============================================================================
# ignore data/ CONTENTS (not the dir itself, so specific subdirs can be re-included below)
data/*
!data/.gitkeep
results/
!results/.gitkeep
logs/
!logs/.gitkeep
*.csv
*.h5
*.onnx
*.pt
*.pth
*.safetensors
*.npz
*.npy
*.zip
# Round 11 — bundled SMALL commercial-OK real-data samples per industry, tracked so the demo + Docker run
# on real data offline (PaySim CC-BY-SA, openSNP CC0 / PGS-Catalog CC-BY, ESA-ADB CC-BY-3.0-IGO). Placed
# LAST so these re-includes win over `data/*` and the `*.csv`/`*.npz` rules above (last match wins).
!data/finance/
!data/genomics/
!data/satellite/
!data/finance/**
!data/genomics/**
!data/satellite/**
# but keep small fixture/sample files explicitly added with `git add -f`

# ============================================================================
#  C++/CUDA build outputs (submodules build in place)
# ============================================================================
*.o
*.a
cmake-build-*/
submodules/**/build/

# ============================================================================
#  Node / web
# ============================================================================
node_modules/
web/dist/
web/node_modules/
web/.vite/
*.local

# ============================================================================
#  Editor / OS
# ============================================================================
.DS_Store
.idea/
.vscode/
*.swp
*~

# Local Claude Code session artifacts / notes
.claude/

# ============================================================================
#  Secrets / local config
# ============================================================================
.env
.env.*
!.env.example
*.key
*.pem

# TypeScript incremental build caches (regenerable; were tracked by mistake)
*.tsbuildinfo

# Trained-model artefacts. Weights live on the Hugging Face Hub at a pinned revision (see
# src/qsh/models/registry.py); only the machine-written metrics file and the generated model
# cards belong in git. `fixtures.npy` in particular holds dataset-derived feature rows and must
# not be redistributed under the training data's licence.
models/**/fixtures.npy

# Full 1000 Genomes dosage matrix (2,504 individuals). Regenerable via
# scripts/data/fetch_g1k_dosages.py; kept local so the repository does not redistribute more
# per-sample genotype data than the demo actually needs.
data/genomics/g1k_dosages_full.tsv
# Same reasoning, PRS313: 2,504 individuals x 313 variants is a 24x larger per-sample genotype
# disclosure than the 400 x 77 slice DATA_LICENSES section 4 analysed and justified. Committing it
# would silently widen that scope, so it stays local; a tracked artefact for PRS313 would need its own
# entry in section 4 and a subset matching the justification's shape.
data/genomics/g1k_dosages_prs313.tsv
# pysam caches remote tabix indexes in the CWD, so a dosage fetch leaves ALL.chr*.tbi in the repo root.
*.vcf.gz.tbi

# Raw training corpora. QSH_DATA_ROOT points here; scripts/train and scripts/prep_model_inputs.py
# read from it. NONE of this may be redistributed -- AMLworld is CDLA-Sharing, ESA-ADB is
# CC BY 3.0 IGO at 3.6 GB, and NASA SMAP/MSL carries no licence statement at all (see
# DATA_LICENSES.md). Only the small derived samples under web/public/data/ are committed.
# Added after `git add -A` swept 3.6 GB of ESA channels into a commit and GitHub rejected the push.
/data-full/

# Throwaway probe artefacts. Three of these were committed in Round 24d - a manifest each for
# `probe`, `probe_s`, `probe_t` - because train_torch writes a manifest wherever it is pointed and
# a diagnostic run points it somewhere temporary. They describe models nothing references.
models/probe*/

# latexmk build byproducts. The PDF is committed deliberately (it is a deliverable); its
# dependency database and file list are per-machine build state and change on every run.
report/*.fdb_latexmk
report/*.fls
